      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Since $n$ leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 6, we can write $n = 6k + 1$ for some integer $k$. \n\nTo find the remainder when $n + 2010$ is divided by 6, we substitute $n = 6k + 1$ into the expression:\n\n\\[\nn + 2010 = (6k + 1) + 2010 = 6k + 2011.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when $6k + 2011$ is divided by 6. Since $6k$ is divisible by 6, we only need to consider the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6:\n\n\\[\n2011 \\div 6 = 335 \\text{ remainder } 1.\n\\]\n\nThis means that $2011 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}$. Therefore,\n\n\\[\n6k + 2011 \\equiv 6k + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the remainder when $n + 2010$ is divided by 6 is $\\boxed{1}$.",
